  "handoff": {
    "summary": "Dense ZZ matrices now use the existing generated fmpz residue exporter for every prime modulus through UINT32_MAX. The published result remains canonical: GF(2) uses M4RI where available, primes below 256 use packed storage, and larger supported primes use generated nmod resources. Huge signed entries, empty rectangular shapes, native-disabled execution, region/resource cleanup, and the correct scalar fallback above UINT32_MAX are covered. Million-entry conversion is approximately four times faster than host materialization.",
    "risks": [
      "The existing declared exporter stops at UINT32_MAX; wider primes remain correct but use the scalar fallback.",
      "The optimized nmod publication path uses the existing Node capability gate, so this lane claims no new WASM resource support.",
      "The refreshed integration base reserves flint_word_prime_matrix_pivots before its companion source lane lands, temporarily blocking the combined build, architecture check, and native suite for reasons outside this lane's claims."
    ],
    "next_steps": [
      "Merge word-prime-pivots-remediation, then rerun the combined build, architecture check, and native suite on the integrated tree.",
      "Extend the shared declared residue export ABI to uint64 and width 8 with Windows and WASM portability before removing the wider-prime fallback.",
      "Retain the focused million-entry benchmark as a conversion performance ratchet."
    ]
  }
